## 📊 Dashboard Analysis

### 1. Attrition Rate by Job Role

The dashboard compares employees who stayed and left across job roles and displays the corresponding attrition rate.

| Job Role | Stayed | Left | Attrition Rate |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| Sales Representative | 230 | 80 | 26.9% |
| Sales Executive | 189 | 57 | 23.1% |
| Research Scientist | 167 | 47 | 21.0% |
| Research Director | 135 | 43 | 24.2% |
| Manufacturing Director | 97 | 30 | 23.6% |
| Manager | 87 | 25 | 22.3% |
| Laboratory Technician | 67 | 20 | 23.0% |
| Human Resources | 49 | 14 | 22.2% |
| Healthcare Representative | 122 | 31 | 20.3% |

---

### 2. Attrition by Overtime

| Overtime | Stayed | Left |
|---|---:|---:|
| No | 844 | 110 |
| Yes | 289 | 127 |

The dashboard displays higher attrition for employees with overtime: **30.6% with overtime vs. 11.9% without overtime**.

This is an observed relationship in the dashboard and should not by itself be interpreted as proof that overtime causes attrition.

---

### 3. Attrition by Department

| Department | Stayed | Left |
|---|---:|---:|
| Human Resources | 27 | 16 |
| Research & Development | 440 | 363 |
| Sales | 84 | 41 |

The dashboard highlights Research & Development as having the highest displayed number of employees who left.

---

### 4. Attrition by Age Group

| Age Group | Stayed | Left |
|---|---:|---:|
| 18–25 | 29 | 44 |
| 26–35 | 490 | 116 |
| 36–45 | 425 | 43 |
| 46–55 | 200 | 26 |
| 56+ | 39 | 8 |

The **26–35** group is the largest displayed age segment.

---

### 5. Attrition by Tenure Group

| Tenure Group | Stayed | Left |
|---|---:|---:|
| 0–2 years | 240 | 102 |
| 3–5 years | 374 | 69 |
| 6–10 years | 393 | 55 |

The dashboard identifies the **0–2 years** group as a key retention area and displays a **42.5% attrition rate** for this group.

---

### 6. Attrition by Gender

The dashboard shows:

- **Male:** 926 employees — 63%
- **Female:** 544 employees — 37%

The dashboard displays a higher attrition comparison for males than females. This is a descriptive result and not a causal conclusion.

---

## 💡 Key Business Insights

- Overall employee attrition is **16.1%**.
- The dashboard states that attrition is **2.3% lower than last year**.
- **Sales Representative** has the highest displayed job-role attrition rate at **26.9%**.
- **Research & Development** has the highest displayed number of employees who left at **363**.
- The **0–2 years** tenure group is a key retention segment, with a displayed attrition rate of **42.5%**.
- The workforce consists of **63% male** and **37% female** employees.
- The dashboard displays higher attrition among employees with overtime: **30.6% vs. 11.9%**.
- The **26–35** age group has the largest displayed employee population.
- The **6–10 years** tenure group has the highest displayed number of employees who stayed.

---

## 🎯 Management Focus Areas

1. Monitor employee retention in **Sales and Research & Development**.
2. Focus on **early-tenure employees**, especially the 0–2 years group.
3. Review workload and overtime patterns during employee-retention analysis.
4. Investigate job roles with higher displayed attrition.
5. Strengthen employee engagement and career-development opportunities.
6. Use department, job role, gender, age and tenure segmentation for targeted HR reviews.
7. Track attrition trends regularly rather than relying on a single reporting period.

